Well, the world is not that simple.  There are actually *two* save file
formats used by NetHack: the original one developed for Unix and a newer one
developed by the Mac team.  The original one assumes that for any two
data objects A and B, the "distance" (roughly (int)&A-(int)&B) is a constant
across different executions of the program.  This works fine for Unix since
the program is loaded in one big chunk into the process virtual space.
Under MacOS (and on the Amiga - we swiped the Mac Team's code for this :-)
this assumption is not valid.  (I'll let the Mac Team explain the Mac if they
want - I don't know the details.)  Since the Amiga scatter loads dozens of
data blocks into memory, the distance can't be assumed to be constant, so the
Unix method doesn't work.  For more details, read the code in src/o_init.c.

